and a big portion of
it was thinking about,

00:21:09.240 --> 00:21:13.250
and anticipating what this
process was gonna be like.

00:21:13.250 --> 00:21:17.993
And I was at the Creative House.

00:21:18.920 --> 00:21:22.810
At one point Mike stopped by,

00:21:22.810 --> 00:21:23.740
and I was talking to him

00:21:23.740 --> 00:21:26.010
about how I was processing everything.

00:21:26.010 --> 00:21:29.930
And I was talking to him about it.

00:21:29.930 --> 00:21:32.960
And then I just said...

00:21:32.960 --> 00:21:35.970
Sometimes you realize you're at a point,

00:21:35.970 --> 00:21:39.310
where you feel emotion
welling up inside of you,

00:21:39.310 --> 00:21:42.807
and you can make a choice, to either like,

00:21:42.807 --> 00:21:45.090
"Oh, my throat's getting tight,

00:21:45.090 --> 00:21:47.044
and I'm feeling kind of tingly.

00:21:47.044 --> 00:21:49.710
I think I might cry."

00:21:49.710 --> 00:21:54.710
And I'd built up to
that in my own process,

00:21:55.310 --> 00:21:58.510
and my journaling that
I've been doing on my own.

00:21:58.510 --> 00:22:00.130
But then I was sharing it with him,

00:22:00.130 --> 00:22:02.330
and I could tell that that was happening.

00:22:02.330 --> 00:22:03.820
And I just made a decision.

00:22:03.820 --> 00:22:06.640
I was like, "You know what,
he's a trusted friend.

00:22:06.640 --> 00:22:07.700
He's here for me."

00:22:07.700 --> 00:22:10.219
If you were there, if you just stopped by,

00:22:10.219 --> 00:22:11.993
I would have done the same thing with you.

00:22:13.460 --> 00:22:18.460
And I just started
crying, and I just said...

00:22:21.990 --> 00:22:24.373
The thing that put me over the edge was,

00:22:25.520 --> 00:22:28.310
I'm gonna miss her so much.

00:22:28.310 --> 00:22:32.800
And I'm getting emotional
now just because I still do.

00:22:32.800 --> 00:22:36.210
But I knew that it was gonna be difficult,

00:22:36.210 --> 00:22:38.360
but it was nice to have this moment,

00:22:38.360 --> 00:22:41.400
where I could just spend a few minutes,

00:22:41.400 --> 00:22:43.980
and just bawl like a baby.

00:22:43.980 --> 00:22:47.070
And Mike's a good guy for that,

00:22:47.070 --> 00:22:50.570
because he's such a good listener.

00:22:50.570 --> 00:22:55.570
And he welcomes sitting
with you in that space.

00:22:58.100 --> 00:22:58.970
And he carries a handkerchief,

00:22:58.970 --> 00:23:00.520
which he uses to wipe your face.

00:23:00.520 --> 00:23:03.670
That he didn't have a hanky,

00:23:03.670 --> 00:23:06.467
but it was just saying it out loud.

00:23:06.467 --> 00:23:11.430
"I'm going to miss her
so much," was cathartic,

00:23:11.430 --> 00:23:15.960
and also scary because it
was still many weeks away.

00:23:15.960 --> 00:23:19.732
And the thing that I'd realized was that,

00:23:19.732 --> 00:23:23.300
it was much more built up
than I had even thought.

00:23:23.300 --> 00:23:25.080
And I likened it to...

00:23:26.650 --> 00:23:29.750
When Christie and I were
engaged, and then we got married.

00:23:29.750 --> 00:23:31.240
She was talking about

00:23:31.240 --> 00:23:34.510
how much of her thoughts and expectations,

00:23:34.510 --> 00:23:37.250
from a young age were geared
towards getting married.

00:23:37.250 --> 00:23:42.250
That was just something that
she, I guess fantasized about.

00:23:42.700 --> 00:23:44.970
She visualized her wedding day.

00:23:44.970 --> 00:23:49.540
And I actually realized
that I did the same thing,

00:23:49.540 --> 00:23:51.730
with Lily leaving home

00:23:51.730 --> 00:23:55.210
and assuming she would go off to college,

00:23:55.210 --> 00:23:58.370
from a young age, just
having this image in my mind.

00:23:58.370 --> 00:24:02.330
And it's a cultural trope, right?

00:24:02.330 --> 00:24:06.640
We watched that animated movie,

00:24:06.640 --> 00:24:09.360
that was eerily similar

00:24:09.360 --> 00:24:11.780
to what we were going through, whatever.

00:24:11.780 --> 00:24:13.100
The Mitchells vs the Machines.

00:24:13.100 --> 00:24:14.110
The Mitchells vs the Machines.

00:24:14.110 --> 00:24:15.320
And that's what the is about,

00:24:15.320 --> 00:24:16.510
and we watched that as a family.

00:24:16.510 --> 00:24:17.980
I might've talked about
it on Ear Biscuits,

00:24:17.980 --> 00:24:19.330
I know I told you about it.

00:24:20.440 --> 00:24:24.420
So the idea of sending, I guess,

00:24:24.420 --> 00:24:26.130
especially your firstborn off to college,

00:24:26.130 --> 00:24:29.730
is something that's really built up.

00:24:29.730 --> 00:24:34.730
And I realized that
definitely for me with her,

00:24:34.860 --> 00:24:39.100
and the nature of our
relationship made that so.

00:24:39.100 --> 00:24:40.865
Because I was at the Creative House.

00:24:40.865 --> 00:24:44.003
I hung a bunch of pictures up in there,

00:24:45.890 --> 00:24:48.310
just to start to put
some finishing touches.

00:24:48.310 --> 00:24:51.253
We put finishing touches
on the Creative House.

00:24:52.330 --> 00:24:54.790
Christie and Jesse both
worked on stuff over there,

00:24:54.790 --> 00:24:57.320
introducing more plants and
everything into the situation,

00:24:57.320 --> 00:24:59.693
because of what we're gonna film.

00:25:01.100 --> 00:25:04.030
And one of the pictures that I put up,

00:25:04.030 --> 00:25:06.113
was a picture of my mom, you've seen it.

00:25:07.050 --> 00:25:08.815
And I got that-

00:25:08.815 --> 00:25:09.775
The one that you found when you were...

00:25:09.775 --> 00:25:10.608
I think you talked about this on the-

00:25:10.608 --> 00:25:14.100
Yeah, 'cause I decided
I didn't get a memento,

00:25:14.100 --> 00:25:15.840
and I went back in my nanny's house,

00:25:15.840 --> 00:25:17.080
to get something from the house

00:25:17.080 --> 00:25:20.060
before it was stripped clean.

00:25:20.060 --> 00:25:21.870
And I took this picture of my mom,

00:25:21.870 --> 00:25:23.730
that I remember always seeing.

00:25:23.730 --> 00:25:25.420
And I hung it up in my office

00:25:25.420 --> 00:25:27.730
with some other pictures in a group.

00:25:27.730 --> 00:25:32.460
I also hung up the piece
of art that was behind me,

00:25:32.460 --> 00:25:34.180
when we did the Split-screen episodes

00:25:34.180 --> 00:25:36.620
of Good Mythical Morning,
during the pandemic.

00:25:36.620 --> 00:25:37.453
So I'm trying to...

00:25:37.453 --> 00:25:40.790
And I purchased a picture
that is a replica,

00:25:40.790 --> 00:25:42.840
of the one that was hanging
over the mantelpiece,

00:25:42.840 --> 00:25:45.510
at my Nana and Papa's house
when I was growing up.

00:25:45.510 --> 00:25:48.150
So like these things that connect to me,

00:25:48.150 --> 00:25:49.423
I wanted to put those up.

00:25:50.350 --> 00:25:52.440
But on this particular day,

00:25:52.440 --> 00:25:55.650
when I was alone processing
how I was feeling,

00:25:55.650 --> 00:25:58.000
and looking at that picture of my mom,

00:25:58.000 --> 00:26:02.370
I realized that that picture of her,

00:26:02.370 --> 00:26:07.320
was when she got engaged to my dad.

00:26:07.320 --> 00:26:10.100
They took an engagement photo
and put it in the paper,

00:26:10.100 --> 00:26:13.150
and did the wedding announcement thing.

00:26:13.150 --> 00:26:14.870
Her parents did that.

00:26:14.870 --> 00:26:19.410
And that was the moment when my mom,

00:26:19.410 --> 00:26:22.280
was leaving home for the
first time, getting married,

00:26:22.280 --> 00:26:25.500
and getting on with her life
separate from her parents.

00:26:25.500 --> 00:26:27.600
And it just hit me like a ton of bricks,

00:26:27.600 --> 00:26:32.600
that looking at that version
of my mom in the picture,

00:26:33.320 --> 00:26:37.830
is the place that Lily's at in life.

00:26:37.830 --> 00:26:39.560
The ages may be a few years difference,

00:26:39.560 --> 00:26:44.560
but the stage of life of
leaving home is the same.

00:26:44.646 --> 00:26:47.640
And I couldn't help, but feel that.

00:26:47.640 --> 00:26:50.650
When I went back into the
house to grab something,

00:26:50.650 --> 00:26:52.650
and that was the picture I was drawn to,

00:26:53.650 --> 00:26:56.040
maybe I'm attaching meaning
to it in retrospect,

00:26:56.040 --> 00:26:57.950
maybe subconsciously
something was going on,

00:26:57.950 --> 00:27:00.100
but I definitely made
this connection that,

00:27:04.230 --> 00:27:09.230
Lily is now that age,
and going through this

00:27:09.420 --> 00:27:12.860
crossing over into this
next phase of her life.

00:27:12.860 --> 00:27:17.410
And it was just a moving
way for me to process,

00:27:17.410 --> 00:27:19.733
and also realized that,

00:27:21.290 --> 00:27:25.720
Lily has her own experience
going in this whole process,

00:27:25.720 --> 00:27:27.370
and then I have my own experience.

00:27:27.370 --> 00:27:29.430
So I started thinking about my mom now,

00:27:29.430 --> 00:27:31.913
and my mom when I went off to college,

00:27:33.170 --> 00:27:37.560
and letting go of her only son.

00:27:37.560 --> 00:27:42.360
And I realized that I had...

00:27:42.360 --> 00:27:45.480
Well that Christie and
I had our own experience

00:27:46.346 --> 00:27:49.470
to go through, our own
journey in this process.

00:27:49.470 --> 00:27:51.400
That was separate from Lilly's.

00:27:51.400 --> 00:27:56.213
And we want to support her and
celebrate her crossing over,

00:27:58.240 --> 00:28:00.200
so to speak, but also wanted to-

00:28:00.200 --> 00:28:01.230
You might wanna use a different-

00:28:01.230 --> 00:28:03.180
Crossing over sounds like she's dying.

00:28:03.180 --> 00:28:04.640
Right.

00:28:04.640 --> 00:28:06.358
Moving out that's nice-

00:28:06.358 --> 00:28:09.972
Moving forward, getting on with her life,

00:28:09.972 --> 00:28:12.560
whatever the most positive
way to think about it.

00:28:12.560 --> 00:28:14.240
And that has been part of the struggle.

00:28:14.240 --> 00:28:16.730
But identifying that,

00:28:16.730 --> 00:28:20.480
I have my own experience in
this that is separate from hers,

00:28:20.480 --> 00:28:21.730
and that is much more closer

00:28:21.730 --> 00:28:23.350
related to Christie's experience.

00:28:23.350 --> 00:28:28.110
And I just wanted to be
committed to being there for it,

00:28:28.110 --> 00:28:29.420
as much as I could.

00:28:29.420 --> 00:28:31.823
Being as present as I could.

00:28:33.130 --> 00:28:35.530
Which is why I've got notes,

00:28:35.530 --> 00:28:37.680
because I've been writing some stuff down,

00:28:37.680 --> 00:28:39.040
that as I've been going through this,

00:28:39.040 --> 00:28:41.333
it's like what does this mean to me?

00:28:44.220 --> 00:28:47.233
And how that's is not just a Lily thing,

00:28:48.220 --> 00:28:49.370
there's a me version of this.

00:28:49.370 --> 00:28:50.420
You know what I'm saying?

00:28:50.420 --> 00:28:52.780
As the weeks continued,

00:28:52.780 --> 00:28:55.360
and it got closer and
closer to her leaving,

00:28:55.360 --> 00:28:58.030
we started to have more conversations,

00:28:58.030 --> 00:28:59.807
about what it was going to be like.

00:28:59.807 --> 00:29:02.360
And Lily didn't wanna
talk about it too much.

00:29:02.360 --> 00:29:04.260
And I could tell it was
because she was anxious.

00:29:04.260 --> 00:29:07.183
So actually having specific conversations,

00:29:07.183 --> 00:29:11.450
how do you visualize the move in day?

00:29:11.450 --> 00:29:14.320
How do you visualize how
we're going to drop you off?

00:29:14.320 --> 00:29:15.980
And it was actually helpful

00:29:15.980 --> 00:29:18.600
because she started saying things...

00:29:20.020 --> 00:29:22.800
Well, one of the things she said was,

00:29:22.800 --> 00:29:25.470
she admitted that she was concerned

00:29:25.470 --> 00:29:27.950
about me being recognized.

00:29:27.950 --> 00:29:28.840
When she moves in.

00:29:28.840 --> 00:29:29.960
Yeah.

00:29:29.960 --> 00:29:34.310
And she mentioned it to Christie first,

00:29:34.310 --> 00:29:35.660
and Christie mentioned it to me.

00:29:35.660 --> 00:29:39.900
So I was able to have more
of a reaction with Christie

00:29:39.900 --> 00:29:42.410
and then figure out how I
going to position myself

00:29:42.410 --> 00:29:43.740
in conversations with Lily.

00:29:43.740 --> 00:29:45.361
And what disguise you were gonna wear.

00:29:45.361 --> 00:29:47.073
And what disguise I was gonna wear.

00:29:47.073 --> 00:29:47.906
Exactly.

00:29:49.510 --> 00:29:51.260
'Cause it did kind of come to that.

00:29:52.100 --> 00:29:56.040
I was like, "I want to do
whatever it takes to support Lily,

00:29:56.040 --> 00:29:58.550
and whatever makes her most comfortable."

00:29:58.550 --> 00:30:01.440
And I do understand that she's going off,

00:30:01.440 --> 00:30:02.530
she's gonna a roommate,

00:30:02.530 --> 00:30:04.600
she's gonna meet people during move in.

00:30:04.600 --> 00:30:07.820
And that first impression is associated,

00:30:07.820 --> 00:30:11.100
if now she's associated with this guy,

00:30:11.100 --> 00:30:14.677
who's famous on the internet.

00:30:14.677 --> 00:30:15.510
"Oh my God!

00:30:15.510 --> 00:30:17.410
I watched your dad growing up."

00:30:17.410 --> 00:30:18.595
I mean, not Ryan Reynolds.

00:30:18.595 --> 00:30:19.428
Not Ryan Reynolds.

00:30:19.428 --> 00:30:20.650
Anything closer around Ryan Reynolds.

00:30:20.650 --> 00:30:22.380
No, not anywhere close.

00:30:22.380 --> 00:30:23.390
But just kinda of like-

00:30:23.390 --> 00:30:25.900
Except in age, and number of kids.

00:30:25.900 --> 00:30:26.733
Right.

00:30:27.920 --> 00:30:29.370
But not really in any-

00:30:29.370 --> 00:30:30.340
I mean Ryan Reynolds,

00:30:30.340 --> 00:30:32.370
he just couldn't help
his daughter move in.

00:30:32.370 --> 00:30:33.570
Like he couldn't do that.

00:30:33.570 --> 00:30:36.260
I mean, it would become Ryan Reynolds day,

00:30:36.260 --> 00:30:38.050
at that particular college.

00:30:38.050 --> 00:30:38.910
That didn't happen with you.

00:30:38.910 --> 00:30:41.350
So I wore a Deadpool costume, complete,

00:30:41.350 --> 00:30:42.513
face mask, everything.

00:30:44.530 --> 00:30:46.397
So when I talked to Lily, I was like,

00:30:46.397 --> 00:30:50.230
"Mom told me about your
concern, and I totally get it."

00:30:50.230 --> 00:30:51.210
And I don't want...

00:30:51.210 --> 00:30:53.030
Let's just have this conversation,

00:30:53.030 --> 00:30:54.570
because I'm on board for this.

00:30:54.570 --> 00:30:55.690
I'm actually on board.

00:30:55.690 --> 00:30:58.350
If I need to just sit in the car,

00:30:58.350 --> 00:31:00.090
or be back at the Airbnb,

00:31:00.090 --> 00:31:01.923
and just be on FaceTime or something.

00:31:01.923 --> 00:31:06.923
I'm willing to do whatever
will make you comfortable."

00:31:07.030 --> 00:31:08.360
But I was really scared,

00:31:08.360 --> 00:31:10.570
because I really wanted to be there.

00:31:10.570 --> 00:31:12.410
And so when I brought it up to her,

00:31:12.410 --> 00:31:16.400
I told her that and she said,
"Oh no, you have to be there."

00:31:16.400 --> 00:31:18.150
And I was like, "Oh, that feels so..."

00:31:18.150 --> 00:31:19.770
I'm so glad she said that.

00:31:19.770 --> 00:31:20.710
But I didn't say that.

00:31:20.710 --> 00:31:21.687
I was like,

00:31:21.687 --> 00:31:25.350
"Okay, cool, then let's talk
about the details of this.

00:31:25.350 --> 00:31:27.370
I can wear a disguise."

00:31:27.370 --> 00:31:29.070
And she's like, "Well, wear a hat.

00:31:29.070 --> 00:31:31.257
And don't wear that mythical hat, dad."

00:31:32.130 --> 00:31:33.924
Sometimes you think you're wearing a hat,

00:31:33.924 --> 00:31:36.020
so people don't recognize
you, and it's a mythical hat.

00:31:36.020 --> 00:31:39.083
Yeah not really smart,
but good point for her.

00:31:39.083 --> 00:31:41.490
And I'm like, "I'll be
wearing a mask the whole time.

00:31:41.490 --> 00:31:45.560
And because that's gonna be the rule,

00:31:45.560 --> 00:31:47.670
and I can also wear
sunglasses the whole time.

00:31:47.670 --> 00:31:49.040
But when we're going
inside, you know what?

00:31:49.040 --> 00:31:50.453
I'll wear contacts.

00:31:51.290 --> 00:31:53.950
And I won't talk a lot."

00:31:53.950 --> 00:31:56.320
You mean you wear contacts,
and not wear glasses.

00:31:56.320 --> 00:31:59.110
I wear contacts, so if I
take my sunglasses off,

00:31:59.110 --> 00:31:59.943
I can see...

00:31:59.943 --> 00:32:01.320
Yeah, I'm not wearing my glasses.

00:32:01.320 --> 00:32:03.290
So it's one less thing that...

00:32:03.290 --> 00:32:04.700
'Cause people have still recognized me

00:32:04.700 --> 00:32:06.380
with a hat and a mask on.

00:32:06.380 --> 00:32:08.180
So again, I wanted to do everything.

00:32:09.270 --> 00:32:14.260
So that was the plan,
and I felt good about it,

00:32:14.260 --> 00:32:15.310
'cause I could still be there.

00:32:15.310 --> 00:32:16.980
So you're gonna be dressed
like the invisible man.

00:32:16.980 --> 00:32:18.853
Yeah. Yeah.

00:32:19.840 --> 00:32:24.530
And then I stole your
idea of making a cameo.

00:32:24.530 --> 00:32:27.500
So Christie and I, and
Christie spearheaded this,

00:32:27.500 --> 00:32:31.800
before we left we had
dinner with some people,

00:32:31.800 --> 00:32:33.540
that were close to Lily.

00:32:33.540 --> 00:32:38.540
And then we surprised
her with a cameo montage,

00:32:38.850 --> 00:32:41.610
of her favorite survivor players,

00:32:41.610 --> 00:32:44.284
over the years, sending her off

00:32:44.284 --> 00:32:48.140
giving her well-wishes
and advice for college.

00:32:48.140 --> 00:32:50.660
Some of them would talk
for like 15 or 20 minutes.

00:32:50.660 --> 00:32:52.170
And she absolutely loved that.

00:32:52.170 --> 00:32:56.400
That was the night before we were leaving,

00:32:56.400 --> 00:32:58.450
and it could have been heavy.

00:32:58.450 --> 00:33:00.020
I mean, it's just a brilliant thing.

00:33:00.020 --> 00:33:03.600
It costs them money to get these cameos.

00:33:03.600 --> 00:33:04.900
But even if you...

00:33:04.900 --> 00:33:06.160
Like piece of advice,

00:33:06.160 --> 00:33:08.600
even if it's just one, is totally worth it

00:33:08.600 --> 00:33:12.730
because she was on cloud
nine the rest of the night,

00:33:12.730 --> 00:33:14.330
when otherwise it would be like,

00:33:15.317 --> 00:33:16.967
"This is my last night in my bed,

00:33:18.130 --> 00:33:19.470
before I go off to college.

00:33:19.470 --> 00:33:21.117
I'm saying goodbye to my cat."

00:33:22.870 --> 00:33:25.960
It was emotional, and it really helped.

00:33:25.960 --> 00:33:28.720
And I added a couple of
hobbits there at the end.

00:33:28.720 --> 00:33:30.380
You got Billy Boyd was in.

00:33:30.380 --> 00:33:31.250
And then...

00:33:32.600 --> 00:33:33.790
Why am I forgetting his name?

00:33:33.790 --> 00:33:35.700
Samwise Gamgee from...

00:33:35.700 --> 00:33:38.450
Also in stranger things and-

00:33:38.450 --> 00:33:40.080
Yeah, Goonies.

00:33:40.080 --> 00:33:41.111
What's his name stand by me. Sean Aston.

00:33:41.111 --> 00:33:43.278
Stand by me, Sean Aston.

00:33:43.278 --> 00:33:44.643
Sean Aston.

00:33:46.054 --> 00:33:48.890
And you can give them information.

00:33:48.890 --> 00:33:52.003
Like you gave all your cameo
people information about Jesse.

00:33:52.003 --> 00:33:53.500
Some of it was false.

00:33:53.500 --> 00:33:54.380
Most of it.

00:33:54.380 --> 00:33:56.560
And so Christie gave true information,

00:33:56.560 --> 00:33:57.830
about where she's going to school,

00:33:57.830 --> 00:33:58.980
and studying and all that stuff.

00:33:58.980 --> 00:34:01.560
So it was cool to have
people talking about it.

00:34:01.560 --> 00:34:04.027
But then for Sean, I said,

00:34:04.027 --> 00:34:07.650
"I want you to make a
cameo for Soccer, the cat,

00:34:07.650 --> 00:34:10.860
because his owner, Lily, my daughter,

00:34:10.860 --> 00:34:13.627
is leaving to go to college
and Soccer can't go."

00:34:14.479 --> 00:34:17.160
At least until her sophomore year,

00:34:17.160 --> 00:34:18.470
if she moves off campus.

00:34:18.470 --> 00:34:21.610
So you need to tell him,
"Hey, she still loves you.

00:34:21.610 --> 00:34:23.500
She's not gonna be here that often,

00:34:23.500 --> 00:34:24.761
but she'll still think about you,

00:34:24.761 --> 00:34:26.230
and she'll FaceTime with you."

00:34:26.230 --> 00:34:27.490
And there's limited characters,

00:34:27.490 --> 00:34:29.310
so I tried to get all that in there.

00:34:29.310 --> 00:34:30.733
And then he never said
one, and he never said one,

00:34:30.733 --> 00:34:34.290
and I was like, "This is just stupid."

00:34:34.290 --> 00:34:39.290
And then I made the cameo
clip, and he wasn't in it.

00:34:39.320 --> 00:34:42.790
And then Lily was so excited
when she was going to bed,

00:34:42.790 --> 00:34:44.890
she was like, "Give me the login to cameo,

00:34:45.840 --> 00:34:49.190
so I can watch these again in my bed."

00:34:49.190 --> 00:34:50.967
And then when I gave her
the login, she was like,

00:34:50.967 --> 00:34:54.420
"There's another one
here from Sean Aston."

00:34:54.420 --> 00:34:56.500
And I'm like, "Yes, he
did it. Let's play it."

00:34:56.500 --> 00:34:57.710
And then we played that one,

00:34:57.710 --> 00:35:01.100
and he got the assignment totally wrong.

00:35:01.100 --> 00:35:03.910
But he's like the sweetest guy,

00:35:03.910 --> 00:35:06.050
he's freaking Samwise Gamgee-

00:35:06.050 --> 00:35:07.730
So how did he interpret it?

00:35:07.730 --> 00:35:11.010
And then he would just like
was talking about college,

00:35:11.010 --> 00:35:14.320
and I think at one point he
thought that Lily was Soccer,

00:35:14.320 --> 00:35:16.290
but then I could tell
in the middle of his...

00:35:16.290 --> 00:35:20.500
And he went for like over
15 minutes, very generous.

00:35:20.500 --> 00:35:22.600
I could tell that he glanced
down at his information,

00:35:22.600 --> 00:35:26.643
and he was like, "I got this all wrong."

00:35:28.060 --> 00:35:30.210
And then he took a breath,

00:35:30.210 --> 00:35:33.213
and then he started talking
to Soccer, and he did it.

00:35:34.079 --> 00:35:36.010
That's great.
And it was amazing.

00:35:36.010 --> 00:35:40.230
And I got to give you credit,
that was a brilliant move,

00:35:40.230 --> 00:35:42.150
and it really worked for her.

00:35:42.150 --> 00:35:43.240
I didn't invent cameo,

00:35:43.240 --> 00:35:46.770
as much as I would like
to take credit for that.

00:35:46.770 --> 00:35:49.360
So we all go as a family.

00:35:49.360 --> 00:35:51.580
And it's a long drive.

00:35:51.580 --> 00:35:52.940
I'm intentionally not talking about

00:35:52.940 --> 00:35:55.963
where Lily's going to college,
because I don't want to,

00:35:58.520 --> 00:36:00.230
and I don't want there
to be any speculation

00:36:00.230 --> 00:36:05.230
about it either, but many
hours away, long drive.

00:36:06.270 --> 00:36:08.030
We're driving at night,

00:36:08.030 --> 00:36:09.800
'cause we had stuff to
shoot during the day,

00:36:09.800 --> 00:36:12.933
and then we all hop in the
car, I'd pack the night before.

00:36:13.830 --> 00:36:18.830
And so the plan was we get
there that night at the Airbnb,

00:36:19.390 --> 00:36:22.700
the next day we move her in,

00:36:22.700 --> 00:36:24.730
but then we're gonna stay
at the Airbnb that night.

00:36:24.730 --> 00:36:25.563
We'll get up in the next...

00:36:25.563 --> 00:36:28.883
She'll stay in her dorm,
get to know her roommates,

00:36:30.280 --> 00:36:32.590
plural, I'll get to that.

00:36:32.590 --> 00:36:36.540
And then the next day we can
get her any supplies she needs,

00:36:36.540 --> 00:36:37.580
we can have dinner.

00:36:37.580 --> 00:36:39.160
We can say goodbye that night.

00:36:39.160 --> 00:36:40.710
We'll still say at the Airbnb,

00:36:40.710 --> 00:36:42.773
after we've said goodbye to Lily.

00:36:43.710 --> 00:36:45.890
We'll get up the next morning,
we'll drive back home.

00:36:45.890 --> 00:36:47.900
So that's what we did, that was the plan.

00:36:47.900 --> 00:36:50.510
We get there the first
night, everybody's exhausted,

00:36:50.510 --> 00:36:51.850
they go to bed at the Airbnb,

00:36:51.850 --> 00:36:56.150
but I had been super
caffeinated for the long drive.

00:36:56.150 --> 00:36:58.840
And so I instituted what
became a nightly ritual,

00:36:58.840 --> 00:37:01.870
which I would go for a walk around...

00:37:01.870 --> 00:37:04.920
It wasn't the college town, but
it was like an adjacent town

00:37:04.920 --> 00:37:07.690
over where we could get an Airbnb.

00:37:07.690 --> 00:37:12.690
So it was 1:00, AM and I'm walking around.

00:37:12.840 --> 00:37:14.030
And again, it's another time

00:37:14.030 --> 00:37:16.720
when I'm just making a decision,

00:37:16.720 --> 00:37:20.570
to process my emotions and my thoughts,

00:37:20.570 --> 00:37:25.570
and memorialize this event for
myself and be present for it.

00:37:27.010 --> 00:37:28.150
And I'm walking around

00:37:28.150 --> 00:37:31.520
this associated town to a college town,

00:37:31.520 --> 00:37:33.620
but not the college town at 1:00 AM.

00:37:33.620 --> 00:37:35.770
And there is nobody there.

00:37:35.770 --> 00:37:39.450
It was the strangest walk
I've ever taken in my life.

00:37:39.450 --> 00:37:42.930
There was nobody, no
signs of life anywhere,

00:37:42.930 --> 00:37:45.360
but there were a good
number of Airbnb's around,

00:37:45.360 --> 00:37:47.890
but I guess no people in them.

00:37:47.890 --> 00:37:52.570
Businesses closed, outdoor
seating for some restaurants

00:37:52.570 --> 00:37:54.373
that weren't put away.

00:37:55.310 --> 00:37:56.143
Nobody.

00:37:56.143 --> 00:38:01.080
I saw a police officer
drive by, and that was it.

00:38:01.080 --> 00:38:02.700
No homeless people.

00:38:02.700 --> 00:38:04.260
I mean, we're used to seeing homeless.

00:38:04.260 --> 00:38:09.050
You walk around, people who
are experiencing homelessness,

00:38:09.050 --> 00:38:10.260
is what you're supposed to call them.

00:38:10.260 --> 00:38:11.840
And they're outside.

00:38:11.840 --> 00:38:12.673
None of that.

00:38:12.673 --> 00:38:13.700
So it was just strange.

00:38:13.700 --> 00:38:15.973
I started taking pictures of nothing.

00:38:16.891 --> 00:38:19.320
It was like an abandoned amusement park.

00:38:19.320 --> 00:38:22.410
It was weird without any rides.

00:38:22.410 --> 00:38:27.410
And then I'm like, "Damn!
There's a statue of a skunk.

00:38:28.140 --> 00:38:29.538
That's weird."

00:38:29.538 --> 00:38:31.045
And then I'm like, "God!"

00:38:31.045 --> 00:38:32.360
'Cause it was like-
That's an actual skunk.

00:38:32.360 --> 00:38:36.010
It was frozen, and it
was totally stretched up.

00:38:36.010 --> 00:38:37.120
A statue of a skunk.

00:38:37.120 --> 00:38:38.630
And it wasn't moving at all.

00:38:38.630 --> 00:38:41.890
And his mouth was open, like
it had been taxidermied.

00:38:41.890 --> 00:38:43.627
And then I realized,

00:38:43.627 --> 00:38:48.280
"Oh my freak! That's a
skunk, a live skunk."

00:38:48.280 --> 00:38:49.790
And I backed away.

00:38:49.790 --> 00:38:52.430
I was as far from me even that camera,

00:38:52.430 --> 00:38:54.410
as it's skunk, and his tail was up,

00:38:54.410 --> 00:38:56.138
but it wasn't facing me yet.

00:38:56.138 --> 00:38:59.950
And I've actually never
walked up on a skunk.

00:38:59.950 --> 00:39:01.080
I just walked up on a skunk,

00:39:01.080 --> 00:39:04.570
literally two nights ago.

00:39:04.570 --> 00:39:06.850
We were at our friend's house together,

00:39:06.850 --> 00:39:08.787
and when we were leaving-
You texted us afterward.

00:39:08.787 --> 00:39:10.547
And he was like,

00:39:10.547 --> 00:39:13.500
"There's a lot of skunks in our
neighborhood in Los Angeles.

00:39:13.500 --> 00:39:15.077
And I was like, "Okay."

00:39:15.077 --> 00:39:16.870
He was like, "You might just watch him

00:39:16.870 --> 00:39:18.270
as you're walking to your car."

00:39:18.270 --> 00:39:20.704
I was like, "This is a weird-"

00:39:20.704 --> 00:39:21.537
You said it was a thoroughfare.

00:39:21.537 --> 00:39:23.080
"This is a weird piece to advice."

00:39:23.080 --> 00:39:24.960
Was it frozen when you saw it?

00:39:24.960 --> 00:39:25.793
Yeah.

00:39:25.793 --> 00:39:28.380
I did not for a moment
think that it was a statue

00:39:28.380 --> 00:39:31.397
or a lawn ornament, but yeah, it was-

00:39:31.397 --> 00:39:33.170
And it was-
Your loss.

00:39:33.170 --> 00:39:35.020
Arched back, tail up,

00:39:35.020 --> 00:39:37.910
facing away from me as I walked past it,

00:39:37.910 --> 00:39:39.260
like ready to squirt.

00:39:39.260 --> 00:39:41.030
Is that correct?

00:39:41.030 --> 00:39:42.150
No.
Spray.

00:39:42.150 --> 00:39:42.983
Spray.

00:39:44.240 --> 00:39:46.050
Ready to spray.

00:39:46.050 --> 00:39:48.347
And so I backed away
and just kept walking,

00:39:48.347 --> 00:39:49.180
but I was like, "He was right, man.

00:39:49.180 --> 00:39:50.070
There's skunk all over this place."

00:39:50.070 --> 00:39:54.523
He walked away, and
then I finished my walk.

00:39:55.470 --> 00:39:58.160
I didn't get too emotional that night,

00:39:58.160 --> 00:40:03.040
'cause I was like, "Oh
tomorrow's the big day."

00:40:03.040 --> 00:40:06.030
We move her in the next afternoon,

00:40:06.030 --> 00:40:10.370
so we had some quality time
together, eating late breakfast.

00:40:10.370 --> 00:40:12.030
There's a whole rigmarole to move her in,

00:40:12.030 --> 00:40:14.370
especially with COVID protocols,

00:40:14.370 --> 00:40:15.890
and stretching people out.

00:40:15.890 --> 00:40:18.970
So you had to sign times.

00:40:18.970 --> 00:40:20.640
There wasn't a swarm of people,

00:40:20.640 --> 00:40:23.990
because they had taken care to isolate.

00:40:23.990 --> 00:40:28.990
Now, Lily had met a
potential roommate online.

00:40:32.670 --> 00:40:33.503
They hit it off,

00:40:33.503 --> 00:40:34.810
so they requested a room together,

00:40:34.810 --> 00:40:38.029
even though she was thinking
about requesting a room alone,

00:40:38.029 --> 00:40:41.423
a single person room
because she's an introvert.

00:40:42.580 --> 00:40:46.410
Well, when her and her
roommate got the assignment,

00:40:46.410 --> 00:40:47.910
they were together,

00:40:47.910 --> 00:40:52.030
but there were also four
other people in the room.

00:40:52.030 --> 00:40:53.580
I'm not talking about a suite,

00:40:53.580 --> 00:40:56.618
I'm talking about one room, one space,

00:40:56.618 --> 00:41:00.860
with loft beds and bunk beds all over it.

00:41:00.860 --> 00:41:05.490
It's like six women in a room together.

00:41:05.490 --> 00:41:08.910
I was like, "Talk about polar
opposite of what you wanted,

00:41:08.910 --> 00:41:09.880
or thought you needed."

00:41:09.880 --> 00:41:11.740
That's intense, man.

00:41:11.740 --> 00:41:13.877
And she had emailed and said,

00:41:13.877 --> 00:41:15.730
"Put me on a wait list
for a smaller room."

00:41:15.730 --> 00:41:16.740
None of that came through.

00:41:16.740 --> 00:41:18.730
So we knew we were
moving in or into a room,

00:41:18.730 --> 00:41:22.303
with all these other people,
and some of them had moved in.

00:41:23.670 --> 00:41:27.150
And so when we moved her stuff in,

00:41:27.150 --> 00:41:31.540
I'm in my getup, mask,
sunglasses, contact lenses, hat.

00:41:31.540 --> 00:41:32.670
Non mythical hat.

00:41:32.670 --> 00:41:34.260
Non mythical hat.

00:41:34.260 --> 00:41:35.890
We put all our stuff in a cart,

00:41:35.890 --> 00:41:37.130
and we take it up to the room.

00:41:37.130 --> 00:41:38.340
We're looking at the
room for the first time.

00:41:38.340 --> 00:41:39.771
You're walking a little bit different?

00:41:39.771 --> 00:41:41.230
Yeah, yeah.

00:41:41.230 --> 00:41:42.270
Not nearly as cool.

00:41:42.270 --> 00:41:44.300
Not Link-
Less cool.

00:41:44.300 --> 00:41:46.460
We get into this room and there are other,

00:41:46.460 --> 00:41:50.970
her roommates, a handful of
'em had already moved in.

00:41:50.970 --> 00:41:54.200
And it was quiet.

00:41:54.200 --> 00:41:56.923
There was no music playing,
nobody was talking.

00:41:57.774 --> 00:41:59.377
And I made up my mind,

00:41:59.377 --> 00:42:04.377
I'm not gonna talk, because
people would recognize my voice.

00:42:05.800 --> 00:42:07.092
And this is something we talked about.

00:42:07.092 --> 00:42:09.370
"I'm gonna keep quiet," is what I said.

00:42:09.370 --> 00:42:12.330
Well, I didn't anticipate
that me keeping quiet

00:42:12.330 --> 00:42:15.430
would really set the tone,

00:42:15.430 --> 00:42:19.010
that when this family
comes in to move Lily in,

00:42:19.010 --> 00:42:20.550
they're really quiet.

00:42:20.550 --> 00:42:22.860
Like Lando didn't talk,
Lincoln didn't talk.

00:42:22.860 --> 00:42:24.780
Then Christie was whispering to Lily.

00:42:24.780 --> 00:42:26.010
And then all of a sudden I realized,

00:42:26.010 --> 00:42:27.370
this is the strangest...

00:42:27.370 --> 00:42:28.800
Like you guys are moving into a museum.

00:42:28.800 --> 00:42:31.726
It felt so weird,

00:42:31.726 --> 00:42:34.840
'cause the girls who were
already in there weren't talking.

00:42:34.840 --> 00:42:37.497
And I felt like there was
this awkwardness of like,

00:42:37.497 --> 00:42:38.780
"Right, another roommates moving in,

00:42:38.780 --> 00:42:39.730
were gonna need to meet her.

00:42:39.730 --> 00:42:43.160
But her parents are here, her
two younger brothers are here.

00:42:43.160 --> 00:42:45.100
It's a little strange."

00:42:45.100 --> 00:42:49.350
But then it basically became silence.

00:42:49.350 --> 00:42:51.500
And that was really strange,

00:42:51.500 --> 00:42:53.190
'cause I didn't wanna be like...

00:42:53.190 --> 00:42:54.937
'Cause what I would've
done normally was like,

00:42:54.937 --> 00:42:56.867
"Hey, it's kind of quiet in here.

00:42:56.867 --> 00:42:58.363
You wanna play some music?

00:42:59.600 --> 00:43:00.840
Everyone can speak freely."

00:43:00.840 --> 00:43:02.870
And I would have embarrassed Lily.

00:43:02.870 --> 00:43:06.980
So it is actually good that
I kept my vow of silence,

00:43:06.980 --> 00:43:10.600
but it permeated in a very weird way,

00:43:10.600 --> 00:43:12.040
that I'll never forget.

00:43:12.040 --> 00:43:13.260
Her move in was just-

00:43:13.260 --> 00:43:14.093
That's weird. Yeah.

00:43:14.093 --> 00:43:14.970
Silent.

00:43:14.970 --> 00:43:17.152
It feels like the other...

00:43:17.152 --> 00:43:17.985
I mean this is the challenge.

00:43:17.985 --> 00:43:18.818
It was like we were sneaking around,

00:43:18.818 --> 00:43:19.680
like we weren't supposed to be there.

00:43:19.680 --> 00:43:21.410
The challenge of that
many people in her room,

00:43:21.410 --> 00:43:23.530
you feel like, "I'm
gonna leave for a while,

00:43:23.530 --> 00:43:25.820
while this other person moves in."

00:43:25.820 --> 00:43:28.300
But you can't really make a policy,

00:43:28.300 --> 00:43:30.430
where you leave the room every time

00:43:30.430 --> 00:43:32.180
things get shaken up a little bit,

00:43:32.180 --> 00:43:34.170
because you'll never be in there.

00:43:34.170 --> 00:43:35.003
Right.

00:43:35.970 --> 00:43:38.310
So we got in and out of there,

00:43:38.310 --> 00:43:41.250
and then it wasn't the big goodbye.

00:43:41.250 --> 00:43:44.670
It was like, "Hey, stay the night.

00:43:44.670 --> 00:43:45.790
We'll pick you up tomorrow,

00:43:45.790 --> 00:43:47.750
we'll go grocery shopping and stuff.

00:43:47.750 --> 00:43:48.583
We'll see you again."

00:43:48.583 --> 00:43:49.990
So it was cool.

00:43:49.990 --> 00:43:52.500
And we had the luxury
with a couple of nights,

00:43:52.500 --> 00:43:54.274
to let her later ease into it.

00:43:54.274 --> 00:43:59.274
So we left and we went back to the Airbnb,

00:44:00.080 --> 00:44:03.577
and that night I went on another walk.

00:44:09.670 --> 00:44:12.070
Before we moved in, we
had written our letters.

00:44:12.070 --> 00:44:14.100
So the first morning we got up,

00:44:14.100 --> 00:44:15.400
Christie and I each wrote her a letter,

00:44:15.400 --> 00:44:17.530
and then we actually got the
boys to write her a letter too.

00:44:17.530 --> 00:44:21.190
And in her journal and stuff,
when she was moving in,

00:44:21.190 --> 00:44:24.093
we put it in a strategic
place where she would find it.

00:44:26.770 --> 00:44:30.870
But then that night after moving her in,

00:44:30.870 --> 00:44:33.380
and her not being at the Airbnb with us,

00:44:33.380 --> 00:44:35.010
I took another walk that night.

00:44:35.010 --> 00:44:39.660
And it was a little bit more emotional.

00:44:39.660 --> 00:44:44.587
I found myself starting to
have dramatic thoughts like,

00:44:44.587 --> 00:44:48.260
"From now on every time Lily
comes home from college,

00:44:48.260 --> 00:44:50.270
it's gonna feel different.

00:44:50.270 --> 00:44:52.090
She's no longer gonna be...

00:44:52.090 --> 00:44:54.538
She's gonna come home as a visitor,

00:44:54.538 --> 00:44:56.930
versus someone who lives here."

00:44:56.930 --> 00:44:58.712
You can only hope.

00:44:58.712 --> 00:45:00.130
It's gonna think...

00:45:00.130 --> 00:45:00.963
Yeah.

00:45:00.963 --> 00:45:01.796
I found myself thinking,

00:45:01.796 --> 00:45:05.320
"Okay, this is a definitive end of an era,

00:45:05.320 --> 00:45:06.440
but the truth is,

00:45:06.440 --> 00:45:08.560
hey, you never know what can happen.

00:45:08.560 --> 00:45:10.910
I mean, pandemic's an example,

00:45:10.910 --> 00:45:12.570
but there's so many
things that could happen.

00:45:12.570 --> 00:45:14.280
It's like, "All right, you know what?

00:45:14.280 --> 00:45:16.530
You are gonna move back
home for a little bit

00:45:17.410 --> 00:45:18.377
at some point."

00:45:19.430 --> 00:45:22.450
But I tend to like, because
I wanna be so present,

00:45:22.450 --> 00:45:26.663
I acknowledge that I tend
to really dramatize things.

00:45:30.060 --> 00:45:32.877
Maybe more than is necessary, but I think,

00:45:32.877 --> 00:45:35.130
"Hey, if that makes it more fun,

00:45:35.130 --> 00:45:37.500
but also more emotional
and more meaningful,

00:45:37.500 --> 00:45:38.927
I just want to be here for it."

00:45:38.927 --> 00:45:41.153
And some of it, maybe I'm conjuring.

00:45:43.170 --> 00:45:46.100
That's just my approach to these things.

00:45:46.100 --> 00:45:47.593
We get up the next day.

00:45:48.520 --> 00:45:50.340
This was the dreaded day,

00:45:50.340 --> 00:45:53.110
that led up to the actual goodbye.

00:45:53.110 --> 00:45:57.763
And so the grocery shopping,
and we went out to dinner.

00:45:59.200 --> 00:46:00.960
I resisted the urge to turn dinner

00:46:00.960 --> 00:46:04.007
into one more big speech moment.

00:46:04.007 --> 00:46:04.890
"Let's go around,

00:46:04.890 --> 00:46:08.530
and everyone tell Lilly
something special about her.

00:46:08.530 --> 00:46:09.880
Let's give her some encouragement,

00:46:09.880 --> 00:46:11.730
about how she's gonna be at college."

00:46:13.760 --> 00:46:14.950
She wouldn't have loved that.

00:46:14.950 --> 00:46:16.787
So we kept it just kind of like,

00:46:16.787 --> 00:46:18.940
"Oh, it felt like a normal dinner."

00:46:18.940 --> 00:46:20.600
We're doing good.

00:46:20.600 --> 00:46:25.600
We get back in the car, and
then Lando just gets emotional.

00:46:27.180 --> 00:46:32.110
And then he's crying
for all the way there.

00:46:32.110 --> 00:46:34.380
He's just crying a lot.

00:46:34.380 --> 00:46:36.270
And it was like, "Poor buddy.

00:46:36.270 --> 00:46:38.120
He's just so sad."

00:46:38.120 --> 00:46:40.193
And that was his moment.

00:46:41.094 --> 00:46:43.240
And then we're standing
out in the parking lot,

00:46:43.240 --> 00:46:47.170
and I picked a place where
there wasn't foot traffic,

00:46:47.170 --> 00:46:49.280
where we could have our moment.

00:46:49.280 --> 00:46:50.363
And it was like,

00:46:52.137 --> 00:46:54.660
"Okay, everybody's got
to say their goodbye.

00:46:54.660 --> 00:46:55.940
And you know what?

00:46:55.940 --> 00:46:58.240
I'm gonna position myself to be last.

00:46:58.240 --> 00:47:00.000
I wanna be last."

00:47:00.000 --> 00:47:01.160
Didn't tell anybody that.

00:47:01.160 --> 00:47:04.250
But Lando was crying, so he went first.

00:47:04.250 --> 00:47:05.460
She's consoling him.

00:47:05.460 --> 00:47:08.350
And Lincoln and Lily
had their inside jokes,

00:47:08.350 --> 00:47:09.400
in saying goodbye.

00:47:09.400 --> 00:47:13.440
And Christie's embracing her
and whispering in her ear.

00:47:13.440 --> 00:47:15.840
And then it's my turn.

00:47:15.840 --> 00:47:17.730
And I embrace her.

00:47:17.730 --> 00:47:20.900
And she's crying, and I'm crying.

00:47:20.900 --> 00:47:23.417
And I'm saying like,

00:47:23.417 --> 00:47:26.470
"You got this, we're so excited for you."

00:47:26.470 --> 00:47:27.757
And then she's like,

00:47:27.757 --> 00:47:30.295
"Mom already said the exact same things."

00:47:31.219 --> 00:47:32.241
I'm like, "Yeah, we're a team."

00:47:32.241 --> 00:47:33.930
That's the problem with going last, man.

00:47:33.930 --> 00:47:34.763
We're a team.

00:47:34.763 --> 00:47:35.950
You gotta brings something
new to the table.

00:47:35.950 --> 00:47:37.260
And then-

00:47:37.260 --> 00:47:39.710
Did you see other groups of parents?

00:47:39.710 --> 00:47:44.000
No, earlier like the day before,

00:47:44.000 --> 00:47:46.220
we saw a couple of tearful goodbyes.

00:47:46.220 --> 00:47:48.170
So yes, we had seen it happen.

00:47:48.170 --> 00:47:49.003
And that's why I was like,

00:47:49.003 --> 00:47:51.390
"I need to pick a better
place in this parking lot,

00:47:51.390 --> 00:47:52.800
where people won't be seeing us,

00:47:52.800 --> 00:47:54.850
'cause I just don't want
that to be a factor."

00:47:54.850 --> 00:47:57.183
So it was nice, it was beautiful.

00:47:59.570 --> 00:48:02.140
It was happy and sad at the same time.

00:48:02.140 --> 00:48:03.080
Christie and I have been talking about

00:48:03.080 --> 00:48:07.130
that Casey Mustgrave song a
lot, and how it really applies.

00:48:07.130 --> 00:48:11.300
That at every moment
of feeling deeply sad,

00:48:11.300 --> 00:48:13.480
there's a companion thought

00:48:13.480 --> 00:48:15.740
of, "This is something to celebrate,

00:48:15.740 --> 00:48:18.040
and this is a new beginning for her.

00:48:18.040 --> 00:48:22.377
And this is an exciting opportunity."

00:48:23.527 --> 00:48:25.060
There's a lot of things
to celebrate, right?

00:48:25.060 --> 00:48:28.063
So it's both at the same time.

00:48:29.250 --> 00:48:34.133
I end my embrace and then she walks away.

00:48:35.630 --> 00:48:37.210
And I'm pulling up camera

00:48:37.210 --> 00:48:39.480
and taking a picture of her walking away.

00:48:39.480 --> 00:48:44.010
And I'm like, "This is the
moment that I've thought about

00:48:44.010 --> 00:48:48.890
Ever since Lilly was born, is
just seeing her walk away."

00:48:48.890 --> 00:48:51.540
And now every time she walks back,

00:48:51.540 --> 00:48:53.220
it's a different arrangement.

00:48:53.220 --> 00:48:55.190
She no longer is under my roof.

00:48:55.190 --> 00:48:56.340
She's no longer...

00:48:58.090 --> 00:49:00.380
Well, I still feel like
she's my responsibility,

00:49:00.380 --> 00:49:03.750
but the terms of our
relationship have shifted.

00:49:03.750 --> 00:49:06.403
The environment is totally different.

00:49:07.310 --> 00:49:11.843
She's crossed over into new life.

00:49:13.710 --> 00:49:14.567
And I go back and I'm like,

00:49:14.567 --> 00:49:17.500
"Man, I really need my walk tonight."

00:49:17.500 --> 00:49:20.850
And I walked back...

00:49:20.850 --> 00:49:24.150
I take my walk, didn't see the skunk.

00:49:24.150 --> 00:49:25.970
And I just found a place to sit down,

00:49:25.970 --> 00:49:30.970
and that's when I just
had this shoulder jumping,

00:49:33.830 --> 00:49:38.817
body heaving, just crying,
just letting it all out.

00:49:40.027 --> 00:49:41.840
And it felt good to be able to do it.

00:49:41.840 --> 00:49:43.510
I felt like I go through
so much of my life,

00:49:43.510 --> 00:49:46.600
knowing that there's something in there

00:49:46.600 --> 00:49:51.600
that I know it hurts, but
it's hard to let it out.

00:49:52.630 --> 00:49:55.573
It's hard not to stop it at the throat.

00:49:57.073 --> 00:50:00.720
And I'm like, "All right,
this is part of it."

00:50:00.720 --> 00:50:04.510
I can't remember the specific
things I was thinking,

00:50:04.510 --> 00:50:05.910
but I need to remember,

00:50:05.910 --> 00:50:08.616
'cause if I have her have to
be an actor and cry again,

00:50:08.616 --> 00:50:11.020
this is the moment I should go to.

00:50:11.020 --> 00:50:12.520
It was that trouble.

00:50:12.520 --> 00:50:14.790
But I think there were some people around,

00:50:14.790 --> 00:50:16.810
'cause this was much
earlier, this wasn't 1:00 AM,

00:50:16.810 --> 00:50:17.870
it wasn't a ghost town.

00:50:17.870 --> 00:50:20.490
And you're just seeing
some dude, just like...

00:50:21.567 --> 00:50:24.230
Just body shaking, crying.

00:50:24.230 --> 00:50:26.260
The Walker. The Walker is out again.

00:50:26.260 --> 00:50:29.794
And then I'm walking, and it was good.

00:50:29.794 --> 00:50:32.350
And again, that was for me.

00:50:32.350 --> 00:50:33.720
Sure I cry on Lily's shoulder,

00:50:33.720 --> 00:50:35.130
when she was crying on my shoulder.

00:50:35.130 --> 00:50:36.390
But it wasn't to the point.

00:50:36.390 --> 00:50:38.890
I wasn't going to be a
wreck in front of her,

00:50:38.890 --> 00:50:41.410
because that's not a
great way to send her off.

00:50:41.410 --> 00:50:42.460
I didn't want her to...

00:50:42.460 --> 00:50:45.020
I didn't want to add one shred of doubt,

00:50:45.020 --> 00:50:47.153
that I'm not gonna be able to handle it.

00:50:48.800 --> 00:50:51.111
But if she would've
seen me at that moment,

00:50:51.111 --> 00:50:51.944
you'd have been like,

00:50:51.944 --> 00:50:54.000
"Oh God, you need to go
to the emergency room?"

00:50:54.000 --> 00:50:56.573
So, I surprised myself.

00:50:58.390 --> 00:51:01.057
And it did feel like an achievement of,

00:51:01.057 --> 00:51:02.807
"Oh God, I got it out."

00:51:04.150 --> 00:51:06.380
And I'm walking back feeling lighter.

00:51:06.380 --> 00:51:07.610
And I took a different route.

00:51:07.610 --> 00:51:11.690
I knew there was this path
around these town homes,

00:51:11.690 --> 00:51:14.110
and this creek that wasn't...

00:51:14.110 --> 00:51:16.570
It was like a dirt path and
I'd walked it during the day.

00:51:16.570 --> 00:51:18.770
And I walked, there was some lighting.

00:51:18.770 --> 00:51:22.360
So I said, "I'm gonna walk
this way back tonight."

00:51:22.360 --> 00:51:23.593
It's my last night here.

00:51:25.810 --> 00:51:30.810
And the lights at first, then
they started to diminish.

00:51:31.380 --> 00:51:33.760
And by the time I got further on the path,

00:51:33.760 --> 00:51:34.950
behind these houses,

00:51:34.950 --> 00:51:38.270
I started to feel like I
was some sort of creeper,

00:51:38.270 --> 00:51:40.530
like I was in people's backyards.

00:51:40.530 --> 00:51:43.220
And it's like, "This look
different during the day.

00:51:43.220 --> 00:51:45.710
But at night I don't feel
like I should be here."

00:51:45.710 --> 00:51:47.628
And I'm starting to have these doubts,

00:51:47.628 --> 00:51:49.730
and there's a lot of darkness on the path.

00:51:49.730 --> 00:51:52.927
And then all of a sudden
I hear somebody say,

00:51:52.927 --> 00:51:54.227
"I mean, no harm."

00:51:56.300 --> 00:51:58.960
And the sound was not coming from...

00:51:58.960 --> 00:51:59.850
That's a great way to-

00:51:59.850 --> 00:52:03.314
A human height, the sound
was coming from the ground.

00:52:03.314 --> 00:52:04.270
Skunk height.

00:52:04.270 --> 00:52:07.590
It was coming from skunk
height in the darkness.

00:52:07.590 --> 00:52:08.423
The talking skunk?

00:52:08.423 --> 00:52:12.450
I recoiled, I didn't say anything.

00:52:12.450 --> 00:52:14.380
I went

00:52:15.710 --> 00:52:20.330
Like a shaggy from Scooby-Doo
pose on the DVD cover.

00:52:20.330 --> 00:52:21.820
He's gotta mean no harm.

00:52:21.820 --> 00:52:24.117
I put my left shoulder forward, and like,

00:52:24.117 --> 00:52:26.150
"Oh, is it a skunk?

00:52:26.150 --> 00:52:28.250
And when I didn't say anything,

00:52:28.250 --> 00:52:30.150
and I still couldn't see anything,

00:52:30.150 --> 00:52:32.133
but it was a person squatting.

00:52:33.991 --> 00:52:36.453
And that person who
meant no harm then said,

00:52:37.510 --> 00:52:38.593
I'm just a woman.

00:52:39.820 --> 00:52:42.880
And I start to look, and I squint my eyes,

00:52:42.880 --> 00:52:47.880
and I saw this puddle.

00:52:48.030 --> 00:52:53.030
And it was a woman squatting
and peeing behind a town home.

00:52:54.490 --> 00:52:57.410
I think she had left the bar or something.

00:52:57.410 --> 00:53:00.090
She had left, she was
on a walk with somebody,

00:53:00.090 --> 00:53:01.840
she had to pee really badly.

00:53:01.840 --> 00:53:04.243
'Cause she was dressed to go out,

00:53:06.373 --> 00:53:09.507
from what I could tell and she had to pee.

00:53:10.730 --> 00:53:11.660
Would you have seen her,

00:53:11.660 --> 00:53:12.740
If she had said nothing?

00:53:12.740 --> 00:53:15.510
I would've walked right over her.

00:53:15.510 --> 00:53:17.550
And that would've really
scared the crap out of me,

00:53:17.550 --> 00:53:18.780
slipping on a woman's peeing.

00:53:18.780 --> 00:53:19.980
Well what did you say?

00:53:21.210 --> 00:53:22.410
She said, "I'm just a woman."

00:53:22.410 --> 00:53:25.640
And I was like, "Oh, it's okay."

00:53:25.640 --> 00:53:28.840
And then she stood up
and she came towards me,

00:53:28.840 --> 00:53:30.240
and then hung a hard left

00:53:30.240 --> 00:53:32.509
and went back up through two town homes.

00:53:32.509 --> 00:53:33.710
Did she finish?

00:53:33.710 --> 00:53:35.440
I didn't ask her that,
but I think she did.

00:53:35.440 --> 00:53:38.050
There was quite a
puddle, a flowing puddle.

00:53:38.050 --> 00:53:41.643
And I was like, "Yeah,
I'm just gotta give...

00:53:41.643 --> 00:53:45.490
You could have done citizen's
arrest for public urination.

00:53:45.490 --> 00:53:46.323
Would've been cool.

00:53:46.323 --> 00:53:48.260
I mean, no harm, I'm just a woman.

00:53:48.260 --> 00:53:49.810
Too late, woman.

00:53:49.810 --> 00:53:52.510
I was standing in the light,
she could see me clearly.

00:53:52.510 --> 00:53:57.400
And she could tell that I was
absolutely terrified of this.

00:53:58.450 --> 00:53:59.283
I don't know.

00:53:59.283 --> 00:54:01.510
It could have been like
a squatting bridge troll

00:54:01.510 --> 00:54:02.430
for all I knew.

00:54:02.430 --> 00:54:03.492
Right.

00:54:03.492 --> 00:54:05.040
Changing its voice.

00:54:05.040 --> 00:54:06.070
Just drooling.

00:54:06.070 --> 00:54:08.367
Lily had asked, she was like,

00:54:08.367 --> 00:54:09.781
"Why don't you come by in the morning

00:54:09.781 --> 00:54:12.270
before you leave town?"

00:54:12.270 --> 00:54:13.980
I'm like, "No, no, no,
no, no, this is it."

00:54:13.980 --> 00:54:16.080
That was before we said goodbye.

00:54:16.080 --> 00:54:17.840
She was trying to delay it.

00:54:17.840 --> 00:54:22.030
And like, "No, no, no, we
have to, we gotta hold firm."

00:54:22.030 --> 00:54:24.600
It's like, "We can't delay this.

00:54:24.600 --> 00:54:27.323
We can't have another
goodbye one more day.

00:54:28.780 --> 00:54:30.800
We'll talk to you, but..."

00:54:30.800 --> 00:54:35.310
And just to reverse a little bit,

00:54:35.310 --> 00:54:37.010
when I was crying and shaking,

00:54:37.010 --> 00:54:39.940
before I saw the woman who
peed and meant no harm...

00:54:42.660 --> 00:54:47.060
After I had a good cry, I
texted Lily and she responded.

00:54:47.060 --> 00:54:50.253
And we had a good exchange, and it was...

00:54:50.253 --> 00:54:53.180
I took a screenshot of
it for my own memory,

00:54:53.180 --> 00:54:56.460
to just be like, "This is the
first conversation we had,

00:54:56.460 --> 00:54:59.180
where she's no longer my daughter."

00:54:59.180 --> 00:55:00.900
I'd think dramatic things like that.

00:55:00.900 --> 00:55:02.770
Of course she's still my daughter.

00:55:02.770 --> 00:55:03.603
It's like, "What's wrong with-"

00:55:03.603 --> 00:55:06.550
You think dramatic things. Really?

00:55:06.550 --> 00:55:09.580
It really helped when we
had that text exchange.

00:55:09.580 --> 00:55:13.760
And the next morning we get
up, we pack up our stuff,

00:55:13.760 --> 00:55:15.483
we've got a whole lot less crap.

00:55:16.357 --> 00:55:20.870
And we took the day
leisurely driving back.

00:55:20.870 --> 00:55:25.870
And now, having been a few weeks
of her being off at school,

00:55:28.630 --> 00:55:30.618
she's off to a really good start.

00:55:30.618 --> 00:55:35.260
She's made friends with her roommates.

00:55:35.260 --> 00:55:38.433
They've had fun together,
she started her classes.

00:55:39.330 --> 00:55:41.493
It's so far so good.

00:55:43.370 --> 00:55:46.277
I can tell she definitely
has her doubts of like,

00:55:46.277 --> 00:55:47.850
"Did I make the right decision?

00:55:47.850 --> 00:55:49.363
Is this too far away?

00:55:50.850 --> 00:55:54.070
I don't have a car, I'm
using public transportation.

00:55:54.070 --> 00:55:56.060
I'm figuring out so things."

00:55:56.060 --> 00:55:56.893
It feels like...

00:55:56.893 --> 00:55:59.000
I remember how it felt
like, we were close to home,

00:55:59.000 --> 00:56:00.290
and we came home every weekend

00:56:00.290 --> 00:56:01.340
stuff that we talked about,

00:56:01.340 --> 00:56:04.527
but still it feels like,

00:56:04.527 --> 00:56:07.160
"Okay, her future hangs in the balance,

00:56:07.160 --> 00:56:08.690
depending on how this goes."

00:56:08.690 --> 00:56:11.590
And I'm not being over dramatic to her,

00:56:11.590 --> 00:56:12.930
again, I keep that to myself,

00:56:12.930 --> 00:56:14.270
and just giving her encouragement.

00:56:14.270 --> 00:56:17.440
But I am encouraged that she's doing well,

00:56:17.440 --> 00:56:19.490
when we FaceTime, she's smiling.

00:56:19.490 --> 00:56:21.430
And she wants to
FaceTime, and she wants...

00:56:23.040 --> 00:56:28.040
And she still misses
us, but it's going good.

00:56:28.290 --> 00:56:29.927
But now we're trying to figure out,

00:56:29.927 --> 00:56:33.030
"All right, how do we
conduct our relationship?

00:56:33.030 --> 00:56:34.160
And what does that look like?

00:56:34.160 --> 00:56:35.680
How much should I be texting?

00:56:35.680 --> 00:56:37.030
And Christie and I are talking,

00:56:37.030 --> 00:56:40.540
how much should we be reaching
out, and pushing for details,

00:56:40.540 --> 00:56:41.860
or letting her reach out to us.

00:56:41.860 --> 00:56:44.113
And there's this dance of figuring out,

00:56:45.152 --> 00:56:48.770
how we conduct our relationship now.

00:56:48.770 --> 00:56:50.520
Because there's some feeling that out,

00:56:50.520 --> 00:56:53.890
and also setting some precedent.

00:56:53.890 --> 00:56:54.723
That like,

00:56:54.723 --> 00:56:59.723
"Okay, we started a new text
chain with the three of us,

00:57:00.060 --> 00:57:04.921
and I'll get mad if Christie
texts her sometimes,

00:57:04.921 --> 00:57:07.270
outside of the chain or vice versa."

00:57:07.270 --> 00:57:08.103
And it's like,

00:57:08.103 --> 00:57:09.640
"Oh, we wanna be included
in the conversation,

00:57:09.640 --> 00:57:14.130
and figuring out, okay, I don't know...

00:57:14.130 --> 00:57:16.060
I don't wanna start asking
a bunch of questions,

00:57:16.060 --> 00:57:17.980
and make her feel like
I'm checking up on her,

00:57:17.980 --> 00:57:20.440
so I'm just gonna send
her a picture of the cat.

00:57:20.440 --> 00:57:22.560
And then if she responds,

00:57:22.560 --> 00:57:24.520
then I'll know that she's in a position,

00:57:24.520 --> 00:57:27.690
where maybe she can talk and
I'll see if she can FaceTime.

00:57:27.690 --> 00:57:31.290
Or other times I find myself...

00:57:31.290 --> 00:57:33.460
Christie and I were talking last night,

00:57:33.460 --> 00:57:37.520
her bedroom in our house is
in the middle of the hallway.

00:57:37.520 --> 00:57:40.233
And so no matter where
you go in the house,

00:57:40.233 --> 00:57:42.470
that's the central point.

00:57:42.470 --> 00:57:45.460
And so we always walk by
it and the door's open,

00:57:45.460 --> 00:57:47.320
and everything's fixed in her room.

00:57:47.320 --> 00:57:50.760
And I just find myself...

00:57:52.500 --> 00:57:53.630
That's the reminder.

00:57:53.630 --> 00:57:55.647
It's like this constant reminder that,

00:57:55.647 --> 00:57:57.897
"Oh, she's not in that room anymore.

00:57:57.897 --> 00:57:59.780
You mean you're not immediately
turning it into a gym?

00:57:59.780 --> 00:58:01.070
I mean, we were joking about

00:58:01.070 --> 00:58:02.550
what we were gonna turn the room into,

00:58:02.550 --> 00:58:05.210
but it's just gonna be there
for her to be the same,

00:58:05.210 --> 00:58:09.430
when she comes home for
Thanksgiving or Christmas break.

00:58:09.430 --> 00:58:11.810
But, yeah, I found
myself going by the room,

00:58:11.810 --> 00:58:14.203
and just walking in there and standing.

00:58:16.424 --> 00:58:19.377
And checking on the litter box.

00:58:19.377 --> 00:58:20.440
"Oh, that's why I'm in here.

00:58:20.440 --> 00:58:23.350
I'm in here to check on
the cat's litter box,

00:58:23.350 --> 00:58:25.480
or if the dog took a shit in there."

00:58:25.480 --> 00:58:26.810
Which he does.

00:58:26.810 --> 00:58:27.643
Yeah. Jasper's got...

00:58:27.643 --> 00:58:29.046
Yeah, he's got some issues.

00:58:29.046 --> 00:58:31.920
When you were hanging out the other day,

00:58:31.920 --> 00:58:33.200
I was gone for a while,

00:58:33.200 --> 00:58:35.550
and then you found me in Lily's room,

00:58:35.550 --> 00:58:37.050
cleaning up the dog shit,

00:58:37.050 --> 00:58:41.253
that I had stepped on, and
almost slipped to the floor.

00:58:42.090 --> 00:58:43.480
But yeah, it's just like...

00:58:44.345 --> 00:58:45.500
I don't know.

00:58:45.500 --> 00:58:46.640
Making decisions is...

00:58:46.640 --> 00:58:50.540
Well, I'm not gonna go and
just stand in her room,

00:58:50.540 --> 00:58:53.120
and just be sad.

00:58:53.120 --> 00:58:55.420
I'm gonna have to channel
that into something.

00:58:58.030 --> 00:59:01.200
But I'm just I'm...

00:59:01.200 --> 00:59:03.630
And I shared this in the Good
Mythical Morning episode,

00:59:03.630 --> 00:59:06.290
where I talked about our matching tattoos,

00:59:06.290 --> 00:59:11.290
but just the fact that when she is 18,

00:59:11.640 --> 00:59:13.460
and she's moving out of the house,

00:59:13.460 --> 00:59:15.440
that we have a relationship,

00:59:15.440 --> 00:59:18.676
where she wants to get a
matching tattoo with me.

00:59:18.676 --> 00:59:20.800
It meant so much.

00:59:20.800 --> 00:59:24.680
And we got so close over the pandemic,

00:59:24.680 --> 00:59:27.127
like late nights on the
weekend when it was just like,

00:59:27.127 --> 00:59:29.550
"I'm gonna hang out and
watch stuff late at night."

00:59:29.550 --> 00:59:33.640
And she's gonna stay up
late, making Mac and cheese.

00:59:33.640 --> 00:59:35.840
And so that's when we connect.

00:59:35.840 --> 00:59:37.320
And, "Hey, I'm watching this,

00:59:37.320 --> 00:59:39.300
you wanna come in here and watch this?

00:59:39.300 --> 00:59:40.410
Let's hang out."

00:59:40.410 --> 00:59:44.893
So over the pandemic, we
really became friends,

00:59:45.730 --> 00:59:50.730
and it is the thing,

00:59:51.152 --> 00:59:54.620
that you hope will happen with your kids.

00:59:54.620 --> 00:59:59.103
And I didn't realize how much
that was my specific goal,

01:00:00.000 --> 01:00:01.640
to state it in terms of her leaving,

01:00:01.640 --> 01:00:05.870
that when she moves out of the house,

01:00:05.870 --> 01:00:09.810
I want our relationship to be such that

01:00:09.810 --> 01:00:13.140
she wants to FaceTime,
she wants to reconnect.

01:00:13.140 --> 01:00:16.330
We still have stuff that we're
talking about that's not...

01:00:16.330 --> 01:00:18.440
It's the nature of the conversations,

01:00:18.440 --> 01:00:23.440
is not just about is
dad checking up on you,

01:00:23.750 --> 01:00:28.000
and trying to get information
out of you and details?

01:00:28.000 --> 01:00:28.833
You know?

01:00:28.833 --> 01:00:29.913
Mm-hm.

01:00:29.913 --> 01:00:31.237
Or she calling me saying,

01:00:31.237 --> 01:00:34.198
"I need money, or I need
this, or I need that."

01:00:34.198 --> 01:00:37.420
Of course we want her to
be able to come to us,

01:00:37.420 --> 01:00:38.300
when she needs stuff,

01:00:38.300 --> 01:00:42.430
but I still want her to
want to have a connection.

01:00:42.430 --> 01:00:46.410
And it's a tremendous
blessing of the pandemic,

01:00:46.410 --> 01:00:49.160
that I think with her being so limited

01:00:49.160 --> 01:00:50.348
in who she could be with,

01:00:50.348 --> 01:00:53.740
The fact that we all found
a way to enjoy each other.

01:00:53.740 --> 01:00:57.210
And it really set us up,

01:00:57.210 --> 01:01:00.647
and gives me hope that
the goodbye was not...

01:01:04.315 --> 01:01:09.315
It's a change of practices
with our relationship,

01:01:09.710 --> 01:01:13.300
but it's not in any way the
end of our relationship,

01:01:13.300 --> 01:01:17.510
that I would sometimes internalize
and dramatize that way.

01:01:17.510 --> 01:01:22.200
So when I go downstairs
for a late night snack,

01:01:22.200 --> 01:01:23.770
and usually she'd be in there,

01:01:23.770 --> 01:01:25.333
and we'd be having a conversation.

01:01:25.333 --> 01:01:29.990
Now, it's like, "Well, I'm gonna
get Lincoln outta his room,

01:01:29.990 --> 01:01:31.387
and get him off of his
video games, and say,

01:01:31.387 --> 01:01:35.010
"Hey son, you gotta step
it up, because Lily's gone.

01:01:35.010 --> 01:01:37.156
You gotta be the friend
that she was to me."

01:01:37.156 --> 01:01:39.380
I've told him that.

01:01:39.380 --> 01:01:43.403
And he's laughed, but
yeah, it is an opportunity.

01:01:44.510 --> 01:01:46.647
I really think about the lesson learned

01:01:46.647 --> 01:01:49.110
with Lily did in a positive way,

01:01:49.110 --> 01:01:52.770
to create me and Lincoln's version of that

01:01:52.770 --> 01:01:54.493
over the next two years.

01:01:55.410 --> 01:01:58.327
But yeah, I go to the pantry and I'm like,

01:01:58.327 --> 01:01:59.580
"There's Lily cereal.

01:01:59.580 --> 01:02:01.100
The one that she said I couldn't have."

01:02:01.100 --> 01:02:03.310
So I'm sending her a text and saying,

01:02:03.310 --> 01:02:04.510
"Do I have your permission

01:02:04.510 --> 01:02:08.590
to eat the rest of your
strawberry, honey bunches of oats?"

01:02:08.590 --> 01:02:09.693
And she's like,

01:02:11.397 --> 01:02:14.330
"No, you gotta save it
for when I come home."

01:02:14.330 --> 01:02:16.490
And she's like, "Just
kidding, you can have it."

01:02:16.490 --> 01:02:19.370
I'm like, "I texted our
picture of the empty bowl,

01:02:19.370 --> 01:02:22.240
and I'm like, "Too late I already ate it."

01:02:22.240 --> 01:02:24.670
So it's just making...

01:02:24.670 --> 01:02:27.190
We're at a phase now what we're
still trying to figure out,

01:02:27.190 --> 01:02:30.033
how do we keep the relationship active,

01:02:31.010 --> 01:02:35.860
and keep that bond going?

01:02:35.860 --> 01:02:40.860
And being able to celebrate
and give her the support,

01:02:42.540 --> 01:02:45.670
and strength she needs
to keep looking forward,

01:02:45.670 --> 01:02:48.290
and not start looking back to us.

01:02:48.290 --> 01:02:49.123
Yeah.

01:02:49.123 --> 01:02:51.773
But that's pretty much the
experience I wanted to share.

01:02:52.610 --> 01:02:54.040
And I'm grateful

01:02:54.040 --> 01:02:57.770
that it has been pretty
heart wrenching for me,

01:02:57.770 --> 01:03:00.220
'cause it just says a lot
about our relationship.

01:03:02.360 --> 01:03:04.653
But it's for the best,
and I believe in it.

01:03:07.000 --> 01:03:08.640
You sound like you're
dealing with it well.

01:03:08.640 --> 01:03:09.550
But it may not...

01:03:09.550 --> 01:03:12.200
And it may be totally
different with the boys.

01:03:12.200 --> 01:03:15.020
Well, what I've been thinking about

01:03:15.020 --> 01:03:16.480
as you've been talking-

01:03:16.480 --> 01:03:17.755
And I'm not saying because they're boys,

01:03:17.755 --> 01:03:20.350
but for all factors.

01:03:20.350 --> 01:03:21.643
I'm different than you,

01:03:22.690 --> 01:03:25.260
Locke is different than Lily.

01:03:25.260 --> 01:03:28.070
Our families are different in some ways.

01:03:28.070 --> 01:03:31.760
One of the things that I think
me and Locke have in common

01:03:31.760 --> 01:03:33.543
and Jesse also,

01:03:35.497 --> 01:03:37.875
we're always thinking about the future.

01:03:37.875 --> 01:03:40.210
We're kind of always on the next page.

01:03:40.210 --> 01:03:41.110
Mm-Hmm, yeah.

01:03:41.110 --> 01:03:43.350
And a lot of times when people
aren't on the next page,

01:03:43.350 --> 01:03:46.100
it's just like, "Why are
you still on this page?

01:03:46.100 --> 01:03:48.080
'Cause we're all already
in the next chapter."

01:03:48.080 --> 01:03:49.290
Right?

01:03:49.290 --> 01:03:51.930
And so Locke has a tendency...

01:03:51.930 --> 01:03:55.080
I mean, if you think about the
way that I treated college,

01:03:55.080 --> 01:03:56.740
do think it had something
to do with the fact that,

01:03:56.740 --> 01:03:58.420
we went to school close?

01:03:58.420 --> 01:03:59.253
Sure.

01:03:59.253 --> 01:04:01.300
But I've ventured to guess that,

01:04:01.300 --> 01:04:04.063
if I had have gone to school a state away,

01:04:05.498 --> 01:04:07.990
it wouldn't have been much different.

01:04:07.990 --> 01:04:10.930
The process of my parents dropping me off,

01:04:10.930 --> 01:04:11.763
would've been like...

01:04:11.763 --> 01:04:14.110
I'm not saying my dad
would've just shaken my hand,

01:04:14.110 --> 01:04:15.520
like in an old fifties movie,

01:04:15.520 --> 01:04:18.240
but it would've been like
the nineties version of that.

01:04:18.240 --> 01:04:20.650
Well, we weren't friends with our parents.

01:04:20.650 --> 01:04:24.593
That was not something that
was a part of the equation.

01:04:25.856 --> 01:04:27.300
And I know you have that.

01:04:27.300 --> 01:04:28.420
Yeah.

01:04:28.420 --> 01:04:29.740
I'm definitely...

01:04:29.740 --> 01:04:34.260
me and Locke are friends,
but he's so much like...

01:04:34.260 --> 01:04:35.900
Well, there's a couple of dynamics.

01:04:35.900 --> 01:04:40.120
One, he's so much like me that we...

01:04:40.120 --> 01:04:42.010
You talk about during the pandemic,

01:04:42.010 --> 01:04:43.940
you and Lilly got close.

01:04:43.940 --> 01:04:44.840
During the pandemic,

01:04:44.840 --> 01:04:46.590
me and Locke being close to each other,

01:04:46.590 --> 01:04:49.800
was actually a huge source of conflict.

01:04:49.800 --> 01:04:53.050
You know him, he's got his
own ideas about everything,

01:04:53.050 --> 01:04:54.210
and he is a challenger.

01:04:54.210 --> 01:04:55.848
He's an Ingram eight.

01:04:55.848 --> 01:04:58.040
Yeah.

01:04:58.040 --> 01:04:59.610
The whole going with the flow,

01:04:59.610 --> 01:05:04.610
or being a part of the program
and a part of the plan.

01:05:04.720 --> 01:05:07.440
But his nature is to figure
out what the plan is,

01:05:07.440 --> 01:05:09.000
and then depart from it.

01:05:09.000 --> 01:05:12.540
And that's a difficult thing as a parent,

01:05:12.540 --> 01:05:15.687
that we are constantly
battling each other, right?

01:05:15.687 --> 01:05:19.117
And so there's a lot
of tension oftentimes,

01:05:24.810 --> 01:05:26.176
in our communication.

01:05:26.176 --> 01:05:27.009
Yeah.

01:05:27.009 --> 01:05:30.740
The good news is that we
communicate very frequently,

01:05:30.740 --> 01:05:32.990
about basically everything.

01:05:32.990 --> 01:05:35.147
So he is not like a lot of
teenagers that are like,

01:05:35.147 --> 01:05:37.370
"I've got all this stuff
that I'm thinking about,

01:05:37.370 --> 01:05:39.650
and doing that you don't
have any idea about."

01:05:39.650 --> 01:05:41.433
It's like we talk about everything.

01:05:44.220 --> 01:05:48.770
But a lot of times there's an
intensity to the conversation,

01:05:48.770 --> 01:05:50.990
and not a, "Hey, we're just chilling."

01:05:50.990 --> 01:05:53.570
We're working through something.

01:05:53.570 --> 01:05:55.557
So I think it's finding
the time to be like,

01:05:55.557 --> 01:06:00.460
"How can we have some hang time,

01:06:00.460 --> 01:06:05.260
some intentional hang time to
lay the groundwork for that,

01:06:05.260 --> 01:06:06.430
for the rest of your life."

01:06:06.430 --> 01:06:07.263
Yeah.

01:06:07.263 --> 01:06:09.333
And what you're saying makes me think of,

01:06:11.390 --> 01:06:14.520
the terms of how Lincoln and I interact,

01:06:14.520 --> 01:06:18.270
and the bond that we have is
different, and it's not fair...

01:06:18.270 --> 01:06:23.270
I was joking and I think that
he knew that I was joking,

01:06:23.580 --> 01:06:24.790
when I'm like, "Hey, you gotta step up,

01:06:24.790 --> 01:06:26.330
'cause Lilly's leaving."

01:06:26.330 --> 01:06:28.177
I actually don't know if he's like,

01:06:28.177 --> 01:06:30.910
"I think that Lily was dad's favorite."

01:06:30.910 --> 01:06:31.743
I don't know.

01:06:32.740 --> 01:06:34.750
I don't think it's to that point.

01:06:34.750 --> 01:06:38.323
Or like I want you to then
fill the hole that she's left,

01:06:39.160 --> 01:06:41.762
because the relationship with each child

01:06:41.762 --> 01:06:43.000
is totally different.

01:06:43.000 --> 01:06:44.546
I mean, the stuff that
Jesse was talking about,

01:06:44.546 --> 01:06:47.113
two episodes ago about parenting.

01:06:48.230 --> 01:06:49.063
Yeah.

01:06:49.063 --> 01:06:50.350
That's ringing in my ears now

01:06:50.350 --> 01:06:54.950
in terms of each child is different,

01:06:54.950 --> 01:06:58.490
and so the nature of your
relationship is different.

01:06:58.490 --> 01:07:01.820
Of course it is, that's
how relationships work.

01:07:01.820 --> 01:07:05.190
It's interesting when you
look at certain milestones

01:07:05.190 --> 01:07:07.020
in the future, and you're like...

01:07:07.020 --> 01:07:08.417
Especially for me, I'm like,

01:07:08.417 --> 01:07:10.920
"I'd like for it to be this way."

01:07:10.920 --> 01:07:13.290
And then I need to acknowledge that,

01:07:13.290 --> 01:07:15.190
first of all, I'm not in charge of it,

01:07:16.672 --> 01:07:18.020
and I don't need to control things,

01:07:18.020 --> 01:07:20.253
to make them the way
that I want them to be.

01:07:21.120 --> 01:07:23.630
But there's certain values

01:07:23.630 --> 01:07:26.540
that I want to build
into our relationship.

01:07:26.540 --> 01:07:28.940
So there's some fundamental truths

01:07:28.940 --> 01:07:30.900
associated with our experience,

01:07:30.900 --> 01:07:34.290
that are different than
me controlling the minutia

01:07:34.290 --> 01:07:37.410
of curating an experience.

01:07:37.410 --> 01:07:42.410
So just like a communication
climate that, "Hey, we're..."

01:07:43.170 --> 01:07:44.773
Like what you're talking about.

01:07:47.420 --> 01:07:49.665
You absolutely know that,
you're not gonna replicate

01:07:49.665 --> 01:07:51.920
my experience with Lilly with Locke.

01:07:51.920 --> 01:07:54.390
And it's a good reminder that,

01:07:54.390 --> 01:07:56.750
I'm not gonna do that with Lincoln either,

01:07:56.750 --> 01:07:59.150
because that doesn't make sense.

01:07:59.150 --> 01:08:01.363
That's not how relationships work.

01:08:01.363 --> 01:08:02.196
Right.

01:08:02.196 --> 01:08:05.720
But as a parent, you do look
forward to things like...

01:08:05.720 --> 01:08:08.590
And I think that for me,

01:08:08.590 --> 01:08:11.740
what I just went through
with sending Lilly off

01:08:13.070 --> 01:08:17.833
was more emotional than
even when she gets married.

01:08:18.720 --> 01:08:19.553
I don't know.

01:08:19.553 --> 01:08:20.386
Did I give that impression?

01:08:20.386 --> 01:08:21.219
Because I actually...

01:08:21.219 --> 01:08:23.500
I could be wrong, but I think that's...

01:08:23.500 --> 01:08:24.610
I definitely think so,

01:08:24.610 --> 01:08:28.380
because the transition
between leaving home,

01:08:28.380 --> 01:08:30.710
and it's much more personal to you.

01:08:30.710 --> 01:08:32.160
But It's not the one that...

01:08:32.160 --> 01:08:35.120
I mean, when you think about
the father of the bride, and...

01:08:35.120 --> 01:08:36.980
Of course I'm gonna be emotional,

01:08:36.980 --> 01:08:39.020
but I just don't think
it will be to the extent

01:08:39.020 --> 01:08:40.340
that I just went through.

01:08:40.340 --> 01:08:42.408
Oh, for the sake of the
wedding guests, I hope not.

01:08:42.408 --> 01:08:43.693
Right.

01:08:44.620 --> 01:08:47.049
What's wrong with his shoulders?

01:08:47.049 --> 01:08:48.499
Does he need to go to the ER?

01:08:51.100 --> 01:08:53.030
He's got speech jammed.

01:08:53.030 --> 01:08:55.280
But as a parent looking for...

01:08:55.280 --> 01:08:56.990
I mean, I start to think about...

01:08:58.850 --> 01:09:01.270
Okay, when all the kids
are out of the house,

01:09:01.270 --> 01:09:04.750
I want them to want to come
them back and go on a vacation.

01:09:04.750 --> 01:09:07.740
I want to go on like a
extended family vacation.

01:09:07.740 --> 01:09:10.652
I want them to bring
their significant others

01:09:10.652 --> 01:09:12.250
back with them,

01:09:12.250 --> 01:09:15.310
and "Hey, we're gonna do
this together, let's go."

01:09:15.310 --> 01:09:16.210
Well, I'll just pay for it,

01:09:16.210 --> 01:09:17.721
and that'll take care of itself.

01:09:18.746 --> 01:09:19.610
And so I visualize these things,

01:09:19.610 --> 01:09:23.780
but ultimately it comes down to,

01:09:23.780 --> 01:09:28.780
you want to cultivate a
meaningful, active relationship,

01:09:29.560 --> 01:09:31.330
and then it takes two to tango.

01:09:31.330 --> 01:09:34.633
You wanna be a re a resource for her,

01:09:35.850 --> 01:09:38.310
because the nature of the
communication with our kids,

01:09:38.310 --> 01:09:39.143
like you said...

01:09:40.970 --> 01:09:45.580
Just simply by way of having a phone,

01:09:45.580 --> 01:09:47.970
and being able to be in the
more constant communication,

01:09:47.970 --> 01:09:49.170
the fact that we know...

01:09:50.010 --> 01:09:52.160
It gets late, and I look at my phone

01:09:52.160 --> 01:09:54.140
to figure out where Locke is.

01:09:54.140 --> 01:09:57.560
Think about how different
of an experience that was

01:09:57.560 --> 01:09:58.483
from what we had.

01:10:00.570 --> 01:10:03.833
We could have been and should
have been dead many times,

01:10:05.130 --> 01:10:06.840
when we were their age,
the stuff that we did.

01:10:06.840 --> 01:10:08.420
Mostly at the bottom of a river.

01:10:08.420 --> 01:10:09.810
Yeah.

01:10:09.810 --> 01:10:12.950
But now it's just like, "Oh, it's 12:30.

01:10:12.950 --> 01:10:13.783
Locke's not home.

01:10:13.783 --> 01:10:17.240
Oh, I see the street
that he's on right now.

01:10:17.240 --> 01:10:18.797
I know whose house he's at."

01:10:20.054 --> 01:10:20.887
Okay.

01:10:20.887 --> 01:10:21.720
You know, it's like-

01:10:21.720 --> 01:10:23.530
We don't have that on
for Lily at this point.

01:10:23.530 --> 01:10:25.030
She didn't want that, and it's like-

01:10:25.030 --> 01:10:26.070
Well, yeah, now that she's 18.

01:10:26.070 --> 01:10:27.530
I would like it.
Now that she's 18.

01:10:27.530 --> 01:10:30.070
But just the being able to be

01:10:30.070 --> 01:10:31.640
in that constant communication,

01:10:31.640 --> 01:10:33.327
is something that now you
have to think about like,

01:10:33.327 --> 01:10:38.240
"Okay, what part of
this am I doing for me,

01:10:38.240 --> 01:10:42.210
and what part am I doing for her?

01:10:42.210 --> 01:10:44.850
'Cause I think about that
whole vacation example,

01:10:44.850 --> 01:10:48.017
and I hear myself, and it feels like,

01:10:48.017 --> 01:10:50.080
"Well, that's a nice dream.

01:10:50.080 --> 01:10:51.200
There's no guarantee of that."

01:10:51.200 --> 01:10:55.680
But it also feels like an
expectation that our kids' lives,

01:10:55.680 --> 01:10:57.360
as they build their own lives,

01:10:57.360 --> 01:11:00.500
are gonna still revolve
around our family unit.

01:11:00.500 --> 01:11:03.743
And I think that's why it's emotional,

01:11:03.743 --> 01:11:05.840
because I know that that's shifting.

01:11:05.840 --> 01:11:09.160
And I know that I took
my immediate family,

01:11:09.160 --> 01:11:12.400
away from our extended family
when we moved out here,

01:11:12.400 --> 01:11:14.960
and they just don't have
the same connection,

01:11:14.960 --> 01:11:16.930
that I hope to maintain with my kids.

01:11:16.930 --> 01:11:19.670
But that made me chasing
them across the country,

01:11:19.670 --> 01:11:21.850
because I know that their lives

01:11:21.850 --> 01:11:23.921
aren't gonna revolve around me as dad.

01:11:23.921 --> 01:11:25.780
It may mean it may mean the
nature of your relationship,

01:11:25.780 --> 01:11:27.920
or the nature of how often

01:11:27.920 --> 01:11:30.930
you see them physically in
the same space, may change.

01:11:30.930 --> 01:11:32.710
I mean, one of the things
that you said earlier,

01:11:32.710 --> 01:11:33.937
you were like,

01:11:33.937 --> 01:11:37.610
"I made sure to not have this
breakdown in front of her."

01:11:37.610 --> 01:11:39.860
Or, "I made sure to kind of
keep those things from her."

01:11:39.860 --> 01:11:42.823
I mean, for better or worse,

01:11:47.327 --> 01:11:48.760
that's not happening.

01:11:48.760 --> 01:11:52.060
Your emotions, and the way
that you're processing it,

01:11:52.060 --> 01:11:56.747
Lilly is 100% feeling that
and interpreting that.

01:11:56.747 --> 01:11:59.550
Every look on Christie's face,

01:11:59.550 --> 01:12:02.940
every change in the tone
of voice, with both of you,

01:12:02.940 --> 01:12:04.718
your kids pick up on all that stuff.

01:12:04.718 --> 01:12:05.551
Sure.

01:12:05.551 --> 01:12:06.940
You can have this illusion in your mind

01:12:06.940 --> 01:12:08.038
that you're keeping it from...

01:12:08.038 --> 01:12:10.285
And boundaries are important,
but what I'm saying is that,

01:12:10.285 --> 01:12:13.470
your kids know everything
that you're feeling,

01:12:13.470 --> 01:12:14.980
and everything that you're thinking.

01:12:14.980 --> 01:12:18.210
And so it's more about managing it

01:12:19.121 --> 01:12:21.480
and keeping yourself in check,

01:12:21.480 --> 01:12:23.887
but you don't begin to believe that like,

01:12:23.887 --> 01:12:24.970
"Oh, she has no..."

01:12:24.970 --> 01:12:28.750
She knows exactly how
you're processing this.

01:12:28.750 --> 01:12:30.230
That doesn't mean you should
do it in front of her.

01:12:30.230 --> 01:12:31.260
I agree with your decision.

01:12:31.260 --> 01:12:33.060
But you're right. You're right.

01:12:33.060 --> 01:12:34.290
But it would've been
The decision-

01:12:34.290 --> 01:12:36.946
That moment that I was talking about,

01:12:36.946 --> 01:12:38.850
I didn't let go, and I'm like,
"I'm gonna miss you so much."

01:12:38.850 --> 01:12:39.850
Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

01:12:39.850 --> 01:12:41.840
You don't wanna make it about self,

01:12:41.840 --> 01:12:43.943
and so separating yourself....

01:12:44.840 --> 01:12:48.640
But I'm saying that in terms
of the emotional quality

01:12:48.640 --> 01:12:50.163
that is coming out.

01:12:51.130 --> 01:12:53.740
We put all our shit onto our kids.

01:12:53.740 --> 01:12:57.320
Just like Jesse said, it's
harm reduction at this point.

01:12:57.320 --> 01:12:58.880
Because everything that we think

01:12:58.880 --> 01:13:01.530
that we're keeping from them
is actually seeping out.

01:13:02.750 --> 01:13:04.110
That doesn't mean you
don't set the boundaries,

01:13:04.110 --> 01:13:05.303
it's just an awareness that like,

01:13:05.303 --> 01:13:10.303
"Oh, she totally knows
how difficult this was."

01:13:11.563 --> 01:13:16.563
I mean, one of the things
I saw in so many families,

01:13:17.650 --> 01:13:22.650
throughout the pandemic
is anxiety over COVID,

01:13:24.440 --> 01:13:27.270
that parents had was transferred

01:13:27.270 --> 01:13:29.690
like an avalanche on the kids.

01:13:29.690 --> 01:13:31.720
Even if you think you're
keeping it from them.

01:13:31.720 --> 01:13:32.650
Right.
You know what I'm saying?

01:13:32.650 --> 01:13:34.020
If you've got anxiety,

01:13:34.020 --> 01:13:36.233
if you were worried constantly about it,

01:13:36.233 --> 01:13:39.563
your kids inherited that and took that on.

01:13:40.824 --> 01:13:44.383
And we were with them so
regularly that you can't help it.

01:13:45.490 --> 01:13:46.323
It's more about being like,

01:13:46.323 --> 01:13:51.260
"Okay, what have I
unintentionally passed on to them?

01:13:51.260 --> 01:13:53.525
Like what emotional qualities
have they picked up,

01:13:53.525 --> 01:13:56.830
and how can I minimize that,

01:13:56.830 --> 01:13:58.500
and undo that as much as I possibly can,

01:13:58.500 --> 01:13:59.680
before they go out into the world

01:13:59.680 --> 01:14:00.790
and put it on other people."

01:14:00.790 --> 01:14:01.623
Yeah.

01:14:01.623 --> 01:14:04.260
And now it's like, "All right,
she is out in the world."

01:14:04.260 --> 01:14:05.507
It definitely feels like,

01:14:05.507 --> 01:14:08.950
"All right, but let's see
what the next four years hold,

01:14:08.950 --> 01:14:13.097
and where she's really out in the world."

01:14:14.499 --> 01:14:17.890
I can feel that there's
another stage of...

01:14:17.890 --> 01:14:20.083
College world is not the real world.

01:14:21.700 --> 01:14:23.908
So that mitigates it too.

01:14:23.908 --> 01:14:24.883
And that's one of the
great things about it.

01:14:24.883 --> 01:14:28.200
Hey, you know what? We've done a good job.

01:14:28.200 --> 01:14:30.360
That's what I tell Christie.

01:14:30.360 --> 01:14:31.773
We're far from perfect,

01:14:32.890 --> 01:14:37.610
but we've succeeded at minimizing harm

01:14:37.610 --> 01:14:41.850
and projecting that to the point where

01:14:43.320 --> 01:14:44.414
she's doing her thing.

01:14:44.414 --> 01:14:45.350
Yeah.

01:14:45.350 --> 01:14:47.050
I'm not worried about Lily.

01:14:47.050 --> 01:14:48.190
I'm not worried about her either.

01:14:48.190 --> 01:14:50.400
I'm worried about you.

01:14:50.400 --> 01:14:51.820
I'm doing good, man.

01:14:51.820 --> 01:14:53.270
I had to get my own cereal.

01:14:53.270 --> 01:14:55.047
I couldn't use that excuse anymore.

01:14:55.047 --> 01:14:56.150
"Well, this is Lily's cereal.

01:14:56.150 --> 01:14:58.060
I just happened to be eating it."

01:14:58.060 --> 01:15:00.030
You got a wreck, this is your wreck.

01:15:00.030 --> 01:15:00.863
I do.

